name: kit-issues-notify

# OPTIONAL. Not copied by SETUP-GUIDE.md's default file list, not wired
# anywhere by default -- copy this in only if you deliberately want an
# automated cross-repo ping to the kit's maintainer when this project's
# docs/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md changes. The default this kit assumes otherwise
# is manual: share that file's new entries with the maintainer yourself
# (see KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md.template's own "How an entry reaches the kit's
# maintainer" section). Nothing about the "record, don't fix" rule in
# AGENT_RULES.md depends on this workflow existing.
#
# REAL SETUP REQUIRED, not a drop-in default: this needs a fine-grained
# GitHub Personal Access Token scoped to EXACTLY ONE repository (the kit
# repo you're reporting to) with ONLY the "Issues: write" permission --
# no other repo, no other permission -- stored as a secret in THIS repo
# named KIT_MAINTAINER_PAT. Without that secret set, this workflow is a
# deliberate, silent no-op (a warning annotation, not a failure) -- it
# will never block a PR over a missing optional secret.
#
# Why a real PAT and not the default `github.token`: the token GitHub
# hands this repo's own Actions runs is scoped to this repo only -- it
# structurally cannot open an issue on a different repository, by
# GitHub's own design, regardless of what permissions this file requests.
#
# PUBLIC-TARGET WARNING: the kit repo this notifies (KIT_REPO below) is PUBLIC.
# The issue this workflow opens there carries your source repository's NAME
# (in its title and body) and a link to the PR. If that repository is private,
# its name and the PR link become public. Only enable this if that is fine, or
# point KIT_REPO at a private repository you control (for example a private
# fork of the kit), which is what this workflow was written for.
#
# Deliberately does NOT copy this file's actual entry text into the
# cross-repo notification -- only a link back to the PR that changed it.
# The kit repo this notifies may be public while the project using it is
# private; auto-copying an entry's content across that boundary would be
# exactly the kind of unreviewed data flow this kit's own "Secrets and
# data" rule in AGENT_RULES.md warns against elsewhere. A link lets the
# maintainer read the real entry with their own judgment about who
# should see it, rather than this workflow making that call for them.
# One real consequence of that choice: if the source repo is private and
# the maintainer isn't already a collaborator on it, the link in the
# notification won't resolve for them -- that's expected, not a bug: it
# means a human conversation is genuinely needed to share the content,
# which this workflow was never meant to replace.
#
# Real security note, stated plainly: whoever can read or change this
# repo's Actions secrets can use this token for anything within its
# granted scope (opening issues on the target repo) -- keep the PAT's
# scope to exactly "Issues: write" on exactly the one target repo,
# nothing broader, and treat it as a real credential, not a convenience
# value.
#
# Edit KIT_REPO below to whichever kit repository you're actually
# reporting to (a fork, a different maintainer's copy) before relying on
# this -- it is not auto-detected.
#
# GITHUB-TO-GITHUB ONLY: this file is a GitHub Actions workflow calling
# the `gh` CLI against GitHub's own API -- it has no equivalent on a
# non-GitHub host. If this project's own VCS is GitLab, Bitbucket, Azure
# DevOps, etc. (see SETUP-GUIDE.md's "A different VCS host" section),
# this specific file doesn't port as-is; re-implement the same intent
# (a same-host CI job that pings the kit repo's issue tracker, using
# that host's own API and secret-storage mechanism) rather than assuming
# this YAML translates.
#
# "REACHES YOU" ISN'T A GUARANTEE, EVEN WHEN THIS IS WIRED UP: a PAT can
# expire, a scope can be wrong from the start, a secret can be renamed
# or removed, or this repo's own Actions can be disabled -- any of those
# makes this degrade to a warning line in a CI log in the DOWNSTREAM
# repo, which the kit's maintainer will never see (they don't watch
# every downstream project's own Actions logs). This workflow existing
# and looking "wired up" is not evidence it's actually reaching anyone;
# don't let it substitute for the human occasionally checking in on
# whether their reports are actually landing.

on:
  pull_request:
    types: [opened, synchronize]
    paths:
      - 'docs/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md'

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  notify-kit-maintainer:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Notify the kit's maintainer of a new/updated entry
        env:
          KIT_MAINTAINER_PAT: ${{ secrets.KIT_MAINTAINER_PAT }}
          KIT_REPO: taseenali/Kit-GO
          SOURCE_REPO: ${{ github.repository }}
          PR_URL: ${{ github.event.pull_request.html_url }}
          ACTOR: ${{ github.actor }}
        run: |
          if [ -z "$KIT_MAINTAINER_PAT" ]; then
            echo "::warning::KIT_MAINTAINER_PAT secret is not set -- skipping automated notification (this is expected unless you deliberately set this up; see this workflow's own header). Share docs/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md with the kit's maintainer manually instead."
            exit 0
          fi

          # Link only, never the entry's own text -- see this file's
          # header for why (a possible private-source/public-target trust
          # boundary this workflow shouldn't cross on its own).
          body=$(printf 'A downstream project (%s) updated its kit-issues log.\n\nPR: %s\nOpened/updated by: @%s\n\nThis notification deliberately does not include the entry'"'"'s own text -- see the PR'"'"'s diff of `docs/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md` for the real content.' "$SOURCE_REPO" "$PR_URL" "$ACTOR")

          GH_TOKEN="$KIT_MAINTAINER_PAT" gh issue create \
            --repo "$KIT_REPO" \
            --title "Kit-issues log update from $SOURCE_REPO" \
            --body "$body" || echo "::warning::Could not open an issue on $KIT_REPO -- check the PAT's scope and expiry. Informational only, does not block this PR."
